otor (mo) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `Foot1_Left_Back` | hcan1 | RX=0x11, TX=0x01 | RX=0x12, TX=0x02 |\n| `Foot2_Right_Back` | hcan1 | RX=0x14, TX=0x04 | RX=0x13, TX=0x03 |\n| `Foot3_Left_Front` | hcan1 | RX=0x18, TX=0x08 | RX=0x17, TX=0x07 |\n| `Foot4_Right_Front` | hcan1 | RX=0x15, TX=0x05 | RX=0x16, TX=0x06 |\n\n---\n\n### 4. INITIALIZATION, ENABLE/DISABLE, AND COMMAND-SENDING CODE PATHS\n\n#### Initialization chain:\n```\nmain.c:108 → Robot_Init()\n  → callback_task.cpp:18 → CAN_Init(&hcan1, CAN1_Callback)   [CAN bus init + callback registration]\n  → callback_task.cpp:20 → dog.Chassis_Init()\n      → robotdog_class.cpp:30-33 → FootX.SingleFoot_Init(...) for all 4 feet\n          → foot_control.cpp:20-21 → mo.Init(...) + mi.Init(...)\n              → Motor_DM.cpp:40 → Class_Motor_DM_Normal::Init() [binds CAN object, sets IDs, limits, method]\n          → foot_control.cpp:25-35 → mo.Set_K_P/K_D + mi.Set_K_P/K_D + control angle/omega/torque defaults\n```\n\n#### Enable/Disable code:\n- **Enable**: `Motor_DM.cpp:237-239` — `CAN_Send_Enter()` sends `{0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xfc}` over CAN\n- **Disable**: `Motor_DM.cpp:246-248` — `CAN_Send_Exit()` sends `{0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xfd}` over CAN\n- **Clear Error**: `Motor_DM.cpp:228-230` — `CAN_Send_Clear_Error()` sends `{0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xfb}`\n- **Save Zero**: `Motor_DM.cpp:255-257` — `CAN_Send_Save_Zero()` sends `{0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xff,0xfe}`\n- **Auto-enable on disconnect**: `Motor_DM.cpp:299-302` — In `RTOS_Send_Callback()` and `Dog_Send_Callback()`, if motor status is `DISABLE`, calls `CAN_Send_Enter()` to re-enable\n\n#### Command-sending paths:\n1. **`Dog_Send_Callback()`** (Motor_DM.cpp:315-339) — Called from `foot_control.cpp:113-114` during each Foot_control() cycle. Constrains all control values, then calls `Output()`.\n2. **`RTOS_Send_Callback()`** (Motor_DM.cpp:285-309) — Alternative periodic send path.\n3. **`Output()`** (Motor_DM.cpp:401-464) — Encodes control data per the selected control method (MIT / Angle-Omega / Omega / EMIT) and calls `CAN_Send_Data()`.\n\n#### Lifecycle monitoring:\n- **`RTOS_100ms_Alive_Callback()`** (Motor_DM.cpp:264-278) — Called every 100ms. If no CAN frame was received since last check, sets `Motor_DM_Status = DISABLE`. Otherwise `ENABLE`.\n\n---\n\n### 5. CAN BUS COMMUNICATION CODE FOR MOTORS\n\n#### Low-level CAN Driver (`drv_can.cpp`):\n\n| Function | Line | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| `CAN_Init()` | 106-158 | Configures two filter banks: Filter 0 (mask 0x7F0 → FIFO0 for IDs 0x200-0x20F), Filter 1 (accept-all → FIFO1). Activates RX FIFO0/1, error, and TX mailbox empty interrupts. Calls HAL_CAN_Start. |\n| `CAN_Send_Data()` | 168-200 | Sends a standard CAN frame with retry (up to 200 attempts ~2ms). Updates TX statistics. |\n| `CAN_Send_Data_Realtime()` | 210-222 | Checks if TX mailbox is available first, no retry. |\n| `HAL_CAN_RxFifo0Callback()` | 297-321 | ISR: reads from FIFO0, calls `manager_obj->Callback_Function()` |\n| `HAL_CAN_RxFifo1Callback()` | 328-352 | ISR: reads from FIFO1, calls `manager_obj->Callback_Function()` |\n| `HAL_CAN_ErrorStatusCallback()` | 360-392 | Handles warning/passive/bus-off with auto-recovery |\n| `CAN_Update_Bus_Status()` | 228-263 | Reads MSR/ESR registers for protocol status |\n| `RTOS_1ms_CAN_Motor_Callback()` | 281-290 | **Currently empty** (commented-out DJI motor send code) |\n\n#### CAN1 Callback → Motor Dispatch (`callback_task.cpp:64-105`):\n\nThe `CAN1_Callback()` function is the **central dispatcher** for all motor CAN RX. It checks `CAN_RxMessage->Header.StdId` against each motor's `Get_CAN_Rx_ID()` and routes to the appropriate motor's `CAN_RxCpltCallback()`:\n\n```\nif (ID == Foot1_Left_Back.mi) → Foot1_Left_Back.mi.CAN_RxCpltCallback()\nif (ID == Foot2_Right_Back.mi) → Foot2_Right_Back.mi.CAN_RxCpltCallback()\nif (ID == Foot3_Left_Front.mi) → Foot3_Left_Front.mi.CAN_RxCpltCallback()\nif (ID == Foot4_Right_Front.mi) → Foot4_Right_Front.mi.CAN_RxCpltCallback()\nif (ID == Foot1_Left_Back.mo) → Foot1_Left_Back.mo.CAN_RxCpltCallback()\nif (ID == Foot2_Right_Back.mo) → Foot2_Right_Back.mo.CAN_RxCpltCallback()\nif (ID == Foot3_Left_Front.mo) → Foot3_Left_Front.mo.CAN_RxCpltCallback()\nif (ID == Foot4_Right_Front.mo) → Foot4_Right_Front.mo.CAN_RxCpltCallback()\n```\n\n**Important note:** Currently only CAN1 callback is implemented. There is no CAN2 callback registered. Also, there is no CAN2 callback function declared (only `CAN1_Callback` exists).\n\n#### CAN Hardware Configuration (`can.c`):\n\n- **CAN1**: PD0 (RX), PD1 (TX) — Prescaler=6, BS1=3TQ, BS2=3TQ → effective baud rate = 42MHz / (6*(1+3+3)) = **1Mbps**\n- **CAN2**: PB5 (RX), PB6 (TX) — Same timing config, but **AutoRetransmission = DISABLE** (vs CAN1's ENABLE)\n- Both have AutoBusOff = DISABLE, all interrupt priorities = 1\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Key Architectural Points\n\n1. **Motor type**: **DM (达妙) motors** — Chinese brushless DC motors with CAN interface. Two protocol modes: \"Normal\" (traditional DM protocol) and \"1-to-4\" (DJI-style daisy-chain).\n\n2. **Each leg has 2 motors** (inner `mi` and outer `mo`), controlled via `Class_SingleDogFoot_DM`.\n\n3. **Control loop**: The 1ms TIM6 interrupt fires `Tim6_Robot_1ms_Callback()` → sets foot XY position → calls `Chassis_Control()` → each foot runs `Foot_control()` → inverse kinematics → `Dog_Send_Callback()` → `Output()` → `CAN_Send_Data()`.\n\n4. **All 8 motors** (4 legs × 2 motors) are on **CAN1** bus. The CAN IDs range from 0x01 to 0x18.\n\n5. **Enable is automatic**: If a motor stops responding, `Dog_Send_Callback()` detects DISABLE status and sends `CAN_Send_Enter()` to re-enable it.",
